      }
    ],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "TÁR"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", a 2022 movie, tells the story of Lydia Tár, a world-famous conductor. She suffers from "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "misophonia",
            "zh": "声音恐惧症"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ": a strong dislike for certain sounds. For Tár, the click of a pen is enough to drive her crazy."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Scientists say this condition affects about 18 per cent of the population, but I beg to differ. I think we all have a little misophonia now and then."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Who can bear the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "slurping",
            "zh": "喝东西时发出的声音"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of soup? I think we should lift the spoon and gently send the liquid into our mouths. But some people like to hold the spoon a few centimetres from their face, blow on the soup and take a loud breath in, making an awful sound. Solid food isn’t much better. I’m surprised at how many people chew with their mouths open. It’s never a pretty sight, but the sound is worse. The mouth, wide open, is like a trumpet, broadcasting the sound of chewing."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "This "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "symphony",
            "zh": "交响乐"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of eating plays everywhere. On the street, some people are like lions in a zoo. They attack their food as if it were trying to escape. And it’s the same at the movies. I love watching films on the big screen, but the experience is often ruined by the sound of someone chewing popcorn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "I know I shouldn’t complain about others. I used to "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "grind",
            "zh": "磨"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " my teeth all night. "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "Fortunately",
            "ref": "v-fortunately"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", I don’t have many teeth left, or I wouldn’t have anyone to share a bed with. And then there’s ice. After finishing a drink, I enjoy chewing on the leftover cubes with the few good teeth I do have. This has a strange effect on my wife, Jocasta. It makes her want to jump up and run for her life, as one might run from a fire."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Are there pleasant sounds? Of course. I love the sizzle of food on a barbecue. But it’s the painful sounds that stay with us. Think of the squeak of a knife on a plate or a nail on a blackboard. We’re all victims of misophonia, aren’t we?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
